"""INT-366 — the quarterly access review, generated by the console (files/08 §2.7).

Acceptance, verbatim (GitHub #593):
  "the console generates the quarterly access review of files/08 §2.7 from
   Keycloak, the role grants and the audit log, and downgrades platform_owner
   and tenant_admin sessions to read-only past 100 days without a review;
   admin_access_review_age_days is emitted"

sec_row: admin-console · legal: 2018-10 (measure 2ç, gap ``access-review-generator``).

What is pinned here:
- **Generated, not assembled.** The document is built from the Keycloak admin
  API (holders, effective realm roles, last login), and from the audit chain
  (each holder's last console action, each section's last countersignature).
  Nothing about it is typed in by hand, and no name or mail address from the
  directory reaches it.
- **Signed and countersigned** (files/08 §3 Access & identity: "generate and
  countersign"). A section's reviewer signs the section's digest; a second
  human countersigns it. ``platform_owner`` needs the countersignature from a
  different organisation — §2.7's "external signature". A digest that no
  longer matches the population is refused rather than signed.
- **Enforced.** A review older than 100 days, or none at all, makes every
  ``platform_owner`` and ``tenant_admin`` session read-only: a mutating route
  reached only through one of those roles is refused, and the refusal is
  audited. Reads keep working, and so does every other role. Tested on a fake
  clock with a one-second near miss on each side of the threshold.
- **Emitted.** ``admin_access_review_age_days`` is catalogued ``live`` and an
  alert pages above 100.
"""

